Slices 11B and the live Watch path expose visible HealthKit scalar context; no feature
calculates medical meaning. Health values remain display/audit context only;
`used_for_escalation` remains `false` in application and database constraints.
Only an authenticated Apple fall event, first-party manual SOS, explicit check-in,
authoritative verification timeout, or authenticated invited-responder
acceptance can drive the implemented transitions.
The normal iOS launch now restores a real Keychain session or presents secure
persona enrollment; fixtures and raw-token profiles require explicit QA launch
arguments. The app now implements the genuine Apple fall callback adapter and
live Watch health stream in source, but it does not claim that Apple granted the
fall entitlement or that a signed physical device produced a callback.
Signed-device HealthKit, Core Location, WatchConnectivity, Keychain, and APNs
evidence remains an explicit external gate.

## Next milestones

The six Wave 2 code lanes are integrated. The next product work is a real
same-budget ACE improvement round and promotion/rollback operator experience,
followed by signed-device and live-infrastructure evidence for Apple,
WatchConnectivity, APNs, Mapbox, vLLM, Docker, and Restricted NemoClaw.

External evidence remains separate from feature code: Apple entitlement and
signed-device callbacks; live HealthKit/WatchConnectivity/APNs behavior; a real
Mapbox request; and live Docker or Restricted NemoClaw runs with model, tool,
file-denial, egress-denial, crash, lease, and cleanup evidence. None of these
gates may be replaced with fixtures or synthetic provider claims.

## Run locally

Python 3.14 is the project runtime. This repository has been smoke-tested against
the locally installed Python 3.14 interpreter; pin to 3.13 only if a later
dependency proves incompatible.

```bash
make install
make test
make dev
```

Agent A3 is opt-in. Before enabling its runtime, install the reviewed agent
dependencies into the same environment:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e '.[agent]'
```

Then choose exactly one runtime in [.env.example](.env.example). Common enabled
settings are the PostgreSQL URL/scope, `VITAL_RELAY_AGENT_SANDBOX`, a 32–64 byte
unpadded-base64url tool signing key, the exact model ID and immutable revision,
and the reviewed model artifact SHA-256. The timeout is optional and must remain
10–300 seconds; policy overrides, when used, must be absolute paths.
Runtime-specific settings are presence-sensitive:

| Selection | Required | Must be absent |
|---|---|---|
| `nemoclaw` | sandbox name, exact TLS tool-proxy URL, exact managed inference URL | Docker vLLM key |
| `docker` | Docker vLLM API key | NemoClaw sandbox name, general tool-proxy URL, general vLLM URL |

Blank unselected variables still count as present and fail startup. Set
`VITAL_RELAY_AGENT_ENABLED=true` only after the selected block is complete.
Startup validates only that runtime and never falls back to the other one.

The Docker gateways use the fixed host aliases `host.docker.internal:8000` for
Vital Relay tools and `host.docker.internal:8001` for vLLM. Both host services
must bind an interface reachable from Docker (typically `0.0.0.0`, with the
host firewall limiting exposure to the local demo). Start the API with:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=backend/src .venv/bin/python -m uvicorn \
  vital_relay.main:create_app --factory --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

Do not run the committed Compose profile directly. Application startup builds,
locks, starts, and validates the exact Docker project; shutdown owns its narrow
cleanup and publishes monotonic evidence. If cleanup is incomplete, retry only
through the retained `agent_sandbox_cleanup_retry` callable so the same runner,
project graph, and staged roots remain authoritative. See the
[Docker operational guide](infrastructure/docker-agent/README.md).

`make test` runs the fast non-database suite. `make test-postgres` additionally
starts an isolated temporary Postgres.app cluster for the focused persistence,
incident, PostGIS dispatch, protocol-presentation, and resolution/revocation
acceptance checks. Testing is intentionally narrow for the hackathon; the real
product path takes priority over exhaustive coverage.

The final Wave 2 fast backend result is
`693 passed, 3 skipped, 45 deselected`; the three optional agent-library checks
require the `agent` extra above. The last focused real PostgreSQL result remains
`44 passed`.

Run the iPhone app separately:

```bash
cd apps/apple
swift test
open VitalRelay.xcodeproj
```

Current combined Apple verification reports `151 tests in 31 Swift Testing
suites` plus `10 XCTest cases` passed. Generic unsigned iOS and watchOS builds
also pass; signing, entitlement approval, and physical-device behavior remain
external gates.

Launch the app with no persona or fixture argument. It restores its one
device-only Keychain session or shows enrollment for a backend URL, persona,
and operator-issued enrollment code. `VITAL_RELAY_API_BASE_URL` is optional and
only pre-fills the enrollment URL:

```text
VITAL_RELAY_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

For direct visual QA only, launch with
`--fixture-dispatch-invited` or `--fixture-response-active`.

The legacy `--live-api`, `--persona-command`, and `--persona-responder` paths
remain explicit QA seams for earlier slices. They require their old raw-token
environment values and a backend composed with the equally explicit
`legacy_persona_auth=True` test/QA option; product mode never accepts those raw
tokens as session authority. A notification or deep link supplies only an
incident/invitation locator and must match the active authenticated responder
session.

The command QA profile uses:

```text
VITAL_RELAY_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
VITAL_RELAY_DEVICE_TOKEN=<local demo device token>
VITAL_RELAY_INCIDENT_ID=<active incident UUID>
VITAL_RELAY_ACCOUNT_NAME=<optional command display name>
```

The responder QA profile uses:

```text
VITAL_RELAY_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
VITAL_RELAY_RESPONDER_ID=<seeded responder UUID>
VITAL_RELAY_RESPONDER_TOKEN=<printed seed/bootstrap token>
VITAL_RELAY_INCIDENT_ID=<invited incident UUID>
VITAL_RELAY_ACCOUNT_NAME=<optional responder display name>
```

The first explicit responder QA launch saves that legacy bounded access profile
separately from the product session and then requests notification permission.
A later push or
`vitalrelay://responder/incidents/{incident_id}/invitations/{invitation_id}`
handoff supplies only incident and invitation IDs; it never carries or replaces
the responder credential. A real HTTPS universal link additionally requires
Associated Domains and a hosted `apple-app-site-association` file.

Cleartext HTTP is accepted only for loopback demo hosts (`localhost`,
`127.0.0.1`, or `::1`). Every non-loopback live service must use HTTPS so the
session access token and SOS location are never sent over remote cleartext
transport.

Live manual SOS uses one bounded foreground Core Location acquisition and fails
closed without a fresh, accurate fix. For a locally contained venue demo only,
add `--demo-venue-location` and explicitly provide
`VITAL_RELAY_DEMO_LATITUDE`, `VITAL_RELAY_DEMO_LONGITUDE`, and
`VITAL_RELAY_DEMO_ACCURACY_M`. No coordinate is silently defaulted. See the
[UI-02 implementation note](docs/implementation-slices/ios-ui-02-live-incident-client.md)
for recovery and storage-namespace settings, and the
[UI-03 implementation note](docs/implementation-slices/ios-ui-03-dispatch-coordination.md)
for dispatch polling and privacy details.

## Run with PostgreSQL

Apply the migration and create one explicit retained demo scope:

```bash
export VITAL_RELAY_DATABASE_URL='postgresql+psycopg://postgres@localhost/vital_relay'

make migrate

.venv/bin/vital-relay-db create-scope \
  --scope 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111 \
  --retention-hours 24

.venv/bin/vital-relay-db seed-response-network \
  --scope 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111 \
  --confirm 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111

.venv/bin/vital-relay-db create-persona-account \
  --scope 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111 \
  --confirm 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111 \
  --persona community \
  --display-name 'Demo community member' \
  --user-id demo-user-1

export VITAL_RELAY_DEMO_SCOPE_ID='11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111'
export VITAL_RELAY_DEVICE_TOKEN='replace-with-a-long-random-demo-device-token'
make dev
```

`seed-response-network` persists two responders and two AEDs at explicit Chicago
Loop demo-venue coordinates. It also provisions their responder persona
accounts and prints each newly rotated enrollment code once. The explicit
`create-persona-account` command does the same for a community identity. Save
those revealed-once outputs for native enrollment; rerunning either operation
rotates the relevant enrollment code. PostgreSQL stores only SHA-256 hashes.

At startup, the configured `VITAL_RELAY_DEVICE_TOKEN` deterministically
provisions the command persona and acts as that account's enrollment code. It
is not accepted as a product API credential. After enrollment, the native app
uses only its issued access token for reads/writes and its refresh token for
rotation/revocation.

To enable Slice 08, first generate a stable server-side encryption key:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -c 'from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())'
```

Then set `VITAL_RELAY_APNS_ENABLED=true` and provide the APNs team ID, key ID,
bundle topic, `.p8` key path, `sandbox` or `production` environment, Fernet key,
and comma-separated responder UUID allowlist shown in [.env.example](.env.example).
The app refuses partial APNs configuration, APNs without PostgreSQL, an empty
allowlist, or an invalid environment. Never commit the `.p8` file, encryption
key, enrollment bootstrap, session token, APNs device token, or legacy QA token.

When `VITAL_RELAY_DATABASE_URL` is absent, the application deliberately uses
the in-memory adapters. When it is present, PostgreSQL and a valid active scope
are mandatory; connection or scope failures never trigger a silent fallback.
In PostgreSQL mode, `/healthz` rechecks the database and scope and returns `503`
after the scope closes or the store becomes unavailable.

The incident API exists only in PostgreSQL mode. Product mode resolves the
session access token carried by the existing community/command
`X-Vital-Relay-Device-Token` header or responder
`X-Vital-Relay-Responder-Token` header, then rechecks persona, account, scope,
resource ownership, responder status, and expiry. There is deliberately no
in-memory or simulated incident substitute.

Persona session and discovery endpoints are:

| Endpoint | Credential | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /v1/persona-sessions` | enrollment header | Bind one account to this installation and issue access/refresh tokens |
| `GET /v1/persona-sessions/current` | bearer access token | Revalidate the current account and session |
| `POST /v1/persona-sessions/{session_id}/rotation` | refresh header | Replace the short-lived access token |
| `DELETE /v1/persona-sessions/{session_id}` | refresh header | Revoke the session idempotently |
| `GET /v1/community/incidents/active` | bearer access token | Discover only the community account's active incidents |
| `GET /v1/responders/me/incidents/active` | bearer access token | Discover only this responder's pending/accepted incidents |
| `GET /v1/command/incidents/active` | bearer access token | Discover active incidents in the command account's demo scope |

Discovery rows are privacy-minimal locators. Selecting a row never grants
authority; every selected feature graph performs its own session and resource
authorization.

Community/command-session dispatch coordination exposes responder role,
qualifications, rank, location freshness, and a coarse distance band, but never
the responder's exact point or the wearer's location. Responder decisions and
accepted-dispatch reads require `X-Vital-Relay-Responder-Token`. Only an active
responder identity with the token bound to the acceptance can read the exact
wearer location and route; marking that responder inactive or resolving the
incident revokes later exact reads with a privacy-preserving `404`.

The iOS community graph calls only the redacted `POST` and `GET` coordination
endpoints with its session access token. Its relay constellation can show
searching, invitation pending, and accepted status plus the public AED; it has
no accepted-dispatch,
exact-coordinate, route, ETA, or responder-token model.

The current `static_venue` route is persisted at acceptance with two walking
legs: responder to AED, then AED to wearer. Its distances are PostGIS
straight-line distances and its duration is a simple walking estimate. It is
not live routing, indoor navigation, traffic-aware directions, or a live ETA.
The accepted dispatch is read from its immutable acceptance snapshot, so later
AED edits do not silently rewrite the assigned coordinate or route.

With `VITAL_RELAY_APNS_ENABLED=true`, invitation creation also commits one
allowlisted logical APNs notification in the same database transaction. The
worker reports only bounded states such as provider accepted, permanently
failed, unavailable, or unknown. Provider acceptance means APNs accepted the
request; it does not prove display or user receipt. Write/read timeouts and
expired leases become terminal unknown outcomes rather than unsafe automatic
retries.

The dispatch sequence is:

| Endpoint | Header | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/dispatch` | community/command access | Rank eligible responders/AEDs and create at most one invitation |
| `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/dispatch` | community/command access | Read redacted coordination state |
| `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/responders/{responder_id}/invitation` | responder access | Read only that responder's coarse invitation and current incident version |
| `POST /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/responders/{responder_id}/response` | responder access | Accept or decline idempotently |
| `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/responders/{responder_id}/dispatch` | accepted responder access | Read the exact accepted assignment and static route |

The responder notification surface is:

| Endpoint | Header | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `PUT /v1/responders/{responder_id}/push-registrations/{installation_id}` | responder access | Create or rotate one allowlisted APNs installation without echoing its token |
| `DELETE /v1/responders/{responder_id}/push-registrations/{installation_id}` | responder access | Revoke the authenticated installation idempotently |
| `GET /v1/responders/{responder_id}/invitations/{invitation_id}/notification` | responder access | Read the bounded receipt for that responder's invitation |

The command resolution surface is:

| Endpoint | Header | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/resolution` | command access | Idempotently `close` or record an external `handoff` for a `response_active` incident |

The request is exactly `schema_version`, `resolution_id`, and `action`. The
server owns the timestamp. Acceptance atomically moves the incident from
`response_active` to `resolved` and appends the transition, timeline entry,
exact retry receipt, and accepted-assignment revocation. An exact retry returns
the original accepted snapshot with `already_processed`; conflicting ID reuse
or a losing resolution returns `409` without a second transition.

Responder acceptance also creates the fixed protocol presentation in the same
transaction as the assignment. The protocol read surface is:

| Endpoint | Header | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/protocol` | command access | Read the immutable command presentation |
| `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/responders/{responder_id}/protocol` | active assigned responder access | Read the same presentation through the responder boundary |

After resolution, command-authorized incident, timeline, and protocol
presentation remain readable for audit. The active-only command dispatch view
does not invent a resolved coordination projection. The accepted responder can
still read only its redacted resolved incident projection; its exact dispatch
and protocol reads return `404`. The native responder graph revalidates before
committing exact data and immediately removes cached coordinates, route, and
protocol, then reconciles the redacted incident when revocation or resolution
is observed.

`fall` maps only to `fall-response` `1.0.0`; `manual_sos` maps only to
`manual-sos-response` `1.0.0`. Their raw packaged JSON bytes are SHA-256 pinned
in an append-only ID/version catalog separate from the active kind mapping, then
reread/validated at startup, selection, and every presentation. Missing,
modified, unknown, or mismatched content fails closed. Revision 0004 also
backfills existing accepted assignments from their protected exact version; an
accepted assignment without a presentation is treated as integrity failure.
The response includes fixed steps, disclaimer, source titles, and links to the
American Red Cross, NHS, and American Heart Association references. See the
[Slice 06 implementation note](docs/implementation-slices/06-fixed-protocol-presentation.md)
for the exact integrity and source boundary.

Protocol selection receives only the persisted `IncidentKind`; it has no health,
diagnosis, responder-observation, prompt, or LLM input. The content is fixed
presentation guidance, not generated advice or clinical interpretation.

Fall verification defaults to 15 seconds and the durable deadline worker polls
once per second. For a demo, override those positive values with
`VITAL_RELAY_VERIFICATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and
`VITAL_RELAY_DEADLINE_POLL_SECONDS`. Responder discovery defaults to a 1,000
meter radius and locations no older than 120 seconds; override them with
`VITAL_RELAY_RESPONDER_RADIUS_M` and
`VITAL_RELAY_RESPONDER_STALE_SECONDS`. See [.env.example](.env.example).

The running backend is version `0.9.0`. A healthy PostgreSQL instance reports
`{"status":"ok","slice":"persona_sessions"}` from `/healthz`.

Use `vital-relay-db preview-reset`, `reset-scope`, or `purge-expired` with both
`--scope` and a matching `--confirm` UUID. Held incident snapshots are preserved
while unheld health context and its idempotency receipts are removed together.
The database foreign key itself prevents a held snapshot, request, or scope from
being deleted behind the retention service.

After enrolling the matching community account, place its short-lived issued
access token in `VITAL_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN`. Then submit the frozen metric and
capability batches and capture a snapshot:

The committed fixtures use readable contract-only device IDs. For a real
persona session, first make local request copies and replace every top-level and
nested `device_id` with the exact lowercase session `installation_id`; keep the
account `user_id` (`demo-user-1` below) unchanged. Do not alter the committed
fixtures or reuse their immutable entity IDs after submitting different content.

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/health/metrics:batch \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Vital-Relay-Device-Token: $VITAL_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN" \
  --data @/tmp/vital-relay-health-metric-batch.session.json

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/health/capabilities:batch \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Vital-Relay-Device-Token: $VITAL_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN" \
  --data @/tmp/vital-relay-health-capability-batch.session.json

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/health/snapshots \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Vital-Relay-Device-Token: $VITAL_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN" \
  --data @contracts/examples/health-snapshot-create-request.json
```

Repeating the snapshot request returns the original immutable capture with HTTP
200. Read it later at
`GET /v1/health/snapshots/dddddddd-dddd-4ddd-8ddd-dddddddddddd`.

Submit a real Apple fall callback using the frozen fixture shape:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/wearable/events \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Vital-Relay-Device-Token: $VITAL_RELAY_ACCESS_TOKEN" \
  --data @/tmp/vital-relay-wearable-event.apple-fall.session.json
```

This backend contract does not itself obtain Apple's entitlement or synthesize a
fall. The Apple client must receive the real callback and truthfully supply the
required availability, entitlement, and authorization metadata. Manual SOS uses
`contracts/examples/wearable-event-request.manual-sos.json` and the same endpoint.

Interactive API documentation is available at `http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs`.

